# Clean Code & Clean Code Guard defaults (user 100126)

On every task until the user says `stop clean-code` or `stop clean-code-guard`.

## Clean Code (Uncle Bob)
- **Meaningful names**: Intention-revealing (`elapsedTimeInDays` not `d`). No uninformative names (`data`, `temp`, `info`, `item`, `obj`, `value`). Class names are nouns (`Customer`), method names are verbs (`postPayment`).
- **Small functions**: Target ≤20 lines, do exactly one thing, one level of abstraction.
- **Parameters**: 0-2 ideal, max 4. At 5, introduce a request/config object (record/DTO). Zero boolean flag arguments (split into two functions).
- **Comments**: Never comment bad code—rewrite it. Comments explain *why*, never *what*. Delete commented-out code and step-marker scaffolding.
- **Error handling**: Exceptions over return codes. Write try-catch first. Never return null; never pass null.
- **Law of Demeter**: No train wrecks (`a.getB().getC().doSomething()`).
- **TDD**: Write failing test first, F.I.R.S.T. principles.

## Clean Code Guard (Pre-delivery self-check)
- **Guard pass**: Re-check diff against imperatives before presenting or committing.
- **SOLID**: Single Responsibility (one actor/stakeholder), Open/Closed (extend via strategy/polymorphism, not type-tag edits), Liskov Substitution (never refuse parent contract or weaken postconditions), Dependency Inversion (abstractions live with client).
- **DRY / KISS / YAGNI**: Knowledge duplication vs text duplication. Sandi Metz re-inline rule. Cyclomatic complexity ≤10, nesting depth ≤5. Zero speculative abstractions or dead config flags.
- **LLM Failure Modes**: Never swallow exceptions in broad catch-alls; never hardcode fake fixture values to fake passing tests; never introduce unverified or hallucinated dependencies.
